# Insurance Brokerage

The industry behind ERP Unite — in plain English.

<div class="byline">Industry primer · how the business actually works · the vocabulary that matters</div>

---

<!-- _class: stat -->

<div class="number">200+ carriers</div>

## one brokerage. all those formats.

A mid-sized brokerage receives commission statements from **two hundred plus carriers** every month. Different formats. Different cadences. Different rules. **This is the world Unite is built for.**

---

# The cast of characters

If you can't name these five players from memory, the rest of the industry won't make sense.

<div class="cols three">
<div class="card">

### **Carrier**
The insurance company. Aetna, Cigna, Humana, MetLife. They underwrite the policy and **pay commission** to the brokerage when policies sell or renew.

</div>
<div class="card">

### **Brokerage**
The agency. Holds licenses, sells policies, manages relationships. **Earns commission from carriers**; pays a slice to producers.

</div>
<div class="card">

### **Producer**
The salesperson. The brokerage's individual licensed agent. **Receives compensation** as their share of commission earned on the policies they sold.

</div>
</div>

<div class="cols two">
<div class="card">

### **Customer**
The end policyholder — could be an employer (in benefits), an individual (in P&amp;C), or a business.

</div>
<div class="card">

### **General Agency (MGA)**
A *wholesale* layer between carrier and retail brokerage. Common when a carrier doesn't want to deal directly with hundreds of small brokerages. Think "master CSP partner."

</div>
</div>

---

<!-- _class: section -->

# How the money flows

Three words: carrier pays brokerage; brokerage pays producer.

---

# The two billing models

Insurance commission flows two ways. Brokerages run both, often at the same time.

<div class="cols two">
<div class="card">

### **Direct billing** (carrier billing)
The customer pays the **carrier** directly. The carrier sends the brokerage a statement showing what commission it earned. The brokerage never touches the customer's money.

*Most common in benefits.*

</div>
<div class="card">

### **Agency billing**
The customer pays the **brokerage**. The brokerage holds the funds in a fiduciary account, pays the carrier its share, keeps the commission.

*Most common in P&amp;C.*

</div>
</div>

> *"Agency billing — who ages you bill them. Where a carrier billing, a direct billing, you just create a relationship between the carrier and the vendor."*
> — Jim B, morning session [020:16]

---

# The commission math — typical P&C example

A new $1,000-premium policy, year 1.

- Carrier pays the brokerage **10%** of premium = **$100** commission
- Brokerage's split with the producer: **50/50** → producer gets **$50**, house keeps **$50**
- Next year, renewal commission drops: carrier pays **5%** = $50
- Producer's renewal split drops too: **25%** = $12.50

> *"In this particular case, it's $1,000. Our commission, our revenue is 10%. It's $100. We're playing 50% with the producer. So we paid them 50 out of the hundred. And then everything else is history."*
> — Jim B [029:28]

*Net: new business pays everyone better. Renewals are how the book sustains itself.*

---

# Splits get layered — quickly

Real life doesn't stop at producer + house. Multiple producers split, referral cuts come off the top, admin fees net out before producer payment.

A $100 commission split through a referral:

- Referral producer takes **10% off the top** (gross): **$10** to them
- Of what remains ($90), primary producer's plan is **20%**: **$18** to them
- House keeps **$72**
- Note: primary did not get $20 — the 20% applies to the **net** after referral, not gross

> *"The overrides are getting paid on, but they don't want to share, but they had to split up. Sort of like, instead of the producer getting 50%, they got 41.38 because that was [the correct split]."*
> — Jim B, afternoon session [006:04]

*Net: a single policy can have three or four people getting paid — and the order of operations matters.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->
<!-- _color: #00B294 -->

# Producer compensation — the system of record

Plans are agreements. Splits happen on every policy. The system has to track both.

---

# Producer Compensation Plan vs. Split — the distinction

Easy to confuse. Critical to keep straight.

<div class="cols two">
<div class="card">

### **Producer Compensation Plan (PCP)**
The *agreement* between a producer and the brokerage. "On new business you get 50%; on renewals you get 25%."

A standing rule. One per producer × product vertical × renewal year.

</div>
<div class="card">

### **Split (a.k.a. Split Intake / SIF)**
The *specific allocation* on one policy. "Alan introduced this customer; he gets 25% of my 50%."

A per-deal, per-policy record. Versioned. Approved separately.

</div>
</div>

> *"Think about the plan as the agreement between the producer and the company, and then the split is on a specific deal."*
> — Jim B [031:53]

*This split-on-top-of-plan model is what makes producer comp so much more complex than payroll.*

---

# Producer payment cadence — the silent retention risk

A brokerage's producers expect to be paid on schedule. Different carriers, different cadences.

- Some carriers pay **monthly**; some pay **twice monthly** (e.g., 15th and last day)
- Some carriers **require** the brokerage to pay producers in the same cycle ("when we pay you, you have to pay your producers")
- After an M&amp;A, acquired producers expect their **historical cadence preserved**

> *"When this was acquired, we have an immediate need: producers must continue receiving payment on the day of the month they're used to. Missing it = they leave."*
> — Jim B, paraphrased [024:42]

*Net: payment cadence is a customer-success metric, not a back-office detail.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->
<!-- _color: #F1554B -->

# Carrier statements — the operational nightmare

Two hundred carriers. Two hundred formats. Two hundred ways to be wrong.

---

# What a statement actually looks like

Statements arrive monthly or quarterly. Format depends entirely on the carrier.

- **PDFs** — most carriers. Often a 200-page bundle with thousands of line items
- **Excel files** — better, but inconsistent column layouts between carriers
- **CSVs** — rare and cherished
- **Web portals** — some carriers expect you to download the statement from their portal

> *"It's not unusual for them to get 200 pages of PDF statements and a check of 4 million."*
> — Jim B [097:48]

*And the brokerage has to reconcile every single line.*

---

# The reconciliation pain — by the numbers

A real industry benchmark: tools like High Radius (a third-party match tool) achieve about a **60% auto-match rate** on payments-to-customers.

- 60% match: "deemed like a success point" — even the best tools leave 40% manual
- Customer name mismatches: brokerage uses DBA name; statement uses legal name; an ampersand makes them differ
- Payment timing: customer pays early or late — $1,000 expected, $0 arrives this month and $2,000 next month
- Missing payment data: carrier statement lists customers; the bank wire doesn't list them. The brokerage matches manually.

> *"Sometimes we call the customer one thing and they call them something else. So the name on the statement is not the same that we have in our system."*
> — Jim B [100:09]

*This is the workflow Unite's Mapping Studio + Cash Application together compress from days to minutes.*

---

# The 50-person AP team

How brokerages handle the reconciliation today.

- **50 people** in Accounts Payable doing nothing but statement reconciliation
- Manual matching of names, amounts, periods
- Tracking learned corrections in spreadsheets so the next month doesn't repeat the same errors
- Estimating revenue ahead of carrier actuals (the close can't wait for the statement)

> *"This is where they have 50 people in AP, in AP to go through these here."*
> — Jim B [097:37]

*The labor cost is the business case. Every percentage point of automation pays back in headcount avoidance or redeployment.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->
<!-- _color: #F2B949 -->

# The Wholesale Layer — General Agencies

The "CSP" of insurance. They sit between carrier and retail.

---

# What a General Agency does

GAs are wholesale distributors. They have a direct relationship with the carrier; retail brokerages buy through them.

- **Carrier doesn't want to deal with 5,000 small agencies** — they pick a GA to wholesale through
- **GA gets an override** (extra commission from the carrier) based on aggregate volume
- **Retail brokerages pay the GA** their commission, and the GA pays the producers
- **GAs grow via M&amp;A** — acquire retail brokerages to consolidate volume, get bigger overrides

> *"Think about this as similar in our world is CSP. There are a lot of smaller Microsoft partners that don't have a direct relationship with Microsoft. They go to one of these other master CSP partners. So basically, they're a wholesale product because Microsoft has decided they don't want to deal with these smaller companies."*
> — Jim B [048:04]

*Same model. Different industry. The mental analogy lands every time.*

---

# The three-tier override flow

When a GA + retail broker + producer all earn from the same policy:

- Carrier pays GA **base commission + override** (the override is the wholesale incentive)
- GA keeps the override entirely; splits base commission with the retail broker
- Retail broker then splits their commission with the producer
- "**Double split**" — both sides have producer payments to manage

> *"The override goes to the GA and the commission is split with the retail brokers. But then the retail brokers, if it's a third party, they care about what they pay the producer."*
> — Jim B [084:26]

*Net: in GA scenarios, the same dollar gets split twice before reaching the producer.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->
<!-- _color: #F1554B -->

# Lines of business — they're not all the same

P&C, employee benefits, wealth & retirement. Each has its own commission math.

---

# Property & Casualty (P&C)

The classic insurance vertical — auto, home, commercial liability.

- Carrier pays a fixed **commission percentage** of premium (e.g., 10%)
- Single payment cycle: new business pays once, renewals pay annually
- Revenue recognition is **at policy binding** — when coverage starts, regardless of payment timing
- Direct billing or agency billing both common

*Net: the simplest vertical. Math is clean. Statement formats are still all over the place.*

---

# Employee Benefits

Health, dental, vision, life, 401(k). The most complex vertical.

- Commission tied to **per-employee, per-month** premium — varies as headcount changes
- Life events (hire, fire, marriage, divorce, plan change) move the number every month
- Revenue is **estimated ahead** because actual carrier remittance lags 2-3 months
- COBRA / continuation costs deduct from commission — third-party costs net against producer pay
- Open enrollment timing rarely aligns with policy renewal — more estimating

> *"Let's say I have 100 employees and I get $1, that's $100. Then I receive the next statement and I get $90 — is this a short payment, or is the actual count down?"*
> — Jim B, paraphrased [098:40]

*Net: the unique pain of benefits is *uncertainty* — you book revenue you don't yet have, then reconcile when it arrives.*

---

# Wealth & Retirement

401(k), asset management, family services, executive life.

- Fees are **% of assets under management** (typical 1-2%) — not commission on a sale
- Statements come quarterly, sometimes ad-hoc on plan changes
- Stricter regulatory environment — **fiduciary obligations** affect how revenue is segregated
- Wealth lines cannot share a legal entity with insurance lines — separate company structures required

*Net: simpler commission math; harder regulatory compliance.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->
<!-- _color: #00B294 -->

# Book of business — the producer's leverage

The thing a producer can take with them.

---

# Why producer retention rules everything

A producer's "book of business" — the customers and policies they own the relationship to — is portable.

- Customers buy from the **producer**, not from the brokerage
- If a producer leaves, **most of their customers follow them** to the next brokerage
- This is why payment delays matter so much: a slighted producer can walk and take revenue with them
- M&amp;A pricing for brokerages is often **a multiple of the book of business** — not of revenue or earnings

> *"The allegiance is to that agent and not to the company. So if that producer decides to go somewhere else, a lot of their clients will just go with them."*
> — Jim B [044:38]

*Net: in brokerage M&A, you're buying producers' loyalty. Anything that erodes that loyalty erodes the asset.*

---

# Why this drives the comp tech

Producer retention shapes every back-office decision.

- **Pay producers fast** — pay-on-receipt minimizes their incentive to leave
- **Pay producers accurately** — disputes destroy trust
- **Pay producers with transparency** — they need to see the math
- **Preserve historical cadence on M&amp;A** — never change a producer's payday

*Net: comp tech isn't about cost control. It's about retention.*

---

<!-- _class: section -->
<!-- _color: #F2B949 -->

# Hidden complexity — the things you only learn after a year

Industry knowledge that doesn't make it into the textbook.

---

# Five gotchas to know about

These come up in every brokerage engagement.

- **COBRA fees deducted from commission, not A/P** — third-party COBRA admin costs net against producer pay rather than running through accounts payable. Decouples timing, complicates audit.
- **Carrier promotion dollars** — "extra $1 per PA policy this quarter." Has to be allocated across producers per their split, retroactively. Not a system feature anywhere.
- **No system link between payment and accrual** — $10,000 paid mid-month against 30 separate accruals. The link is reconstructed manually at close.
- **Bulk rate changes aren't really bulk** — even "raise renewal rate from 5% to 6% on all PA policies" requires per-policy review because producer mix varies.
- **Carriers don't agree on data shape** — same customer named three different ways across three statements. Manual normalization required.

> *"Every time you think that I got it now, there's just another thing that just calls YouTube. So don't, don't get fooled. This is complicated stuff."*
> — Jim B [108:55]

---

# Why brokerages M&A so aggressively

The industry consolidates because the back-office becomes the differentiator.

- Brokerages have **PE-backed acceleration strategies** — buy small, buy fast
- Acquisition value is in the **book of business** + the producer relationships
- Post-acquisition, the back-office systems must absorb the new company without breaking what producers expect
- **Infrastructure becomes the bottleneck** — the firms growing fast have outgrown their spreadsheets

> *"They're all going through M&A. They all have incredible PE backing, and they all have acceleration strategies that are being held back by their infrastructure."*
> — Jim B, paraphrased [027:00]

*Net: every brokerage growing past a certain size needs better tooling. That's the market for Unite.*

